# Zylab7 – Prefix Sums with a Procedure Call

This is the Gradescope autograder for Zylab7. Students write a `main` that fills array `B` so that `B[i]` is the sum of the first `i+1` elements of `A`, and an `nSum` function that computes each of those sums. The point of the lab is the procedure call, so a plain loop that skips `nSum` scores zero.

## How it grades

The grader builds array `A` in memory, puts its address in `$s0`, the address of `B` in `$s1` and the length `n` in `$s2`, then runs the instructor's solution and the student's submission through the same harness. After each run it reads back the `n` words at `B`'s original address and compares them.

There are 4 hidden tests, 1 point each. Only the contents of `B` are checked.

| Test | A addr | B addr | n  | A                                     | Expected B                                              |
|------|-------:|-------:|---:|---------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|
| 1    | 4000   | 8000   | 5  | 10, 5, -5, -2, 0                      | 10, 15, 10, 8, 8                                        |
| 2    | 13800  | 13804  | 1  | -8                                    | -8                                                      |
| 3    | 8040   | 8000   | 10 | -5, 8, 4, -6, 6, 0, -3, -2, 2, -2     | -5, 3, 7, 1, 7, 7, 4, 2, 4, 2                           |
| 4    | 2648   | 2648   | 8  | -80, -40, 25, 20, -15, 10, -8, -4     | -80, -120, -175, -355, -745, -1465, -2948, -5892        |

Test 4 has `B` at the same address as `A`, so each store into `B` overwrites an element of `A`. The assignment says to use the updated values in that case, which is what the instructor's solution does since `nSum` reads from memory every time. A submission that caches `A` up front will get a different answer here.

## Addresses

Students work with nominal addresses like 4000 and 8000. Those aren't valid in SPIM, so the harness maps both arrays into a 20000-byte buffer while keeping the same distance between them. Adjacent or overlapping arrays behave exactly as they would at the real addresses.

Right before the student's code runs the harness also zeroes `$t0`–`$t9`, `$a0`–`$a3`, `$v0` and `$v1`. The array setup leaves values in the temporaries otherwise, and code that assumes a fresh simulator (the instructor's solution included) would misbehave.

## Assignment rule

If there is no `jal` in the submission, the autograder sets all four tests to 0. Comments are ignored for this check.

The student sees a one-line note on each test saying the score was changed because of an assignment rule. The reason is written to a separate "Instructor Notes" test entry that is hidden from students and shown with a red banner. It's green when the check passes. The same text is printed at the top of the hidden stdout log.

## Files

- `grader.py` – the autograder
- `MIPSSolution.asm` – instructor's reference solution
- `setup.sh` – installs SPIM inside the Gradescope container
- `run_autograder` – entry point Gradescope calls

## What students should submit

Just the MIPS instructions. `$s0`, `$s1` and `$s2` are already set and `A` is already in memory. The harness provides `.data`, `.text`, `.globl main` and `main:` and strips them from the submission if present, so a full QtSpim program still grades. A `Main:` label with a capital M is left alone.

Two things that will break a submission:

- Using `$at`. SPIM reserves it for the assembler. Use any other register.
- Ending with an exit syscall. The harness prints `B` after the student's code, so exiting early means nothing is printed.

## Testing locally

Make a `submission` folder next to `grader.py`, put a `.asm` file in it and run `python3 grader.py`. Results land in `./results/results.json` and the instructor log prints to the terminal. Needs `spim` installed.
